# Fixed defect 545, 2026-10-09 (panel 201's spec-warden). Symptom:
# `ident<i64>(3)` was told *nothing named `i64` is in scope*, a true
# sentence that never names the rule the program broke: a generic
# function's type arguments are never written at the call (spec § 9). Cause:
# the call parses as two comparisons, `ident < i64 > (3)`, so the type
# stands where a value does and the resolver told it as an unknown name.
# Repair: a type word read as a name, standing in `<…>` written right after
# a name, alone or inside `[…]`, `{…: …}` or before `?`, is told by its
# rule (`resolve/type_argument.hero`); the code stays `unknown_name`.
# The shapes: one type argument, two after a UFCS call, an array's, a map's
# two, a spaced `< i64 >`; and `a < i64`, a comparison, keeps its message.

function ident<A>(x: A) -> A
    return x

function double(x: i64) -> i64
    return x * 2

function main()
    ns: [i64] = [1, 2]
    a: i64 = 2
    print(ident < i64 > 3)  #~ unknown_name
    print(ns.map < i64, i64 > double.len())  #~ unknown_name unknown_name
    print(ident < [i64] > ns.len())  #~ unknown_name
    print(ident < {str: bool} > {"a": true}.len())  #~ unknown_name unknown_name
    print(ident < i64 > 3)  #~ unknown_name
    print(a < i64)  #~ unknown_name
